<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banu Hassan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hassane, Hassani Arabs, Beni Hassan Arabs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hassani <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, or Banu Hassan, were a branch of the Maqil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> who moved into the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> from the 14th century. Their victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zawaya">Zawaya</a> scholarly tribes in the Char Bouba war of the 1670s established a warrior aristocracy over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, which founded the Trarza and Brakna <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a>. Their dialect, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassaniya-arabic">Hassaniya</a>, became the region's main language.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Beni Hassan were one of the four sub-tribes of the Banu Maqil, who came to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> in the 11th century alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-sulaym">Banu Sulaym</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> to punish the Zirids for turning to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. The Maqil probably came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>; they claimed descent from Ja'far ibn Abi Talib, a claim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> rejected. The tradition of the Beni Hassan traces them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a>, though they were Sunni Muslims.</p><p>Under the protection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hilal">Banu Hilal</a> the Maqil wandered between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moulouya-river">Moulouya River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> oases, and in the 13th century they held southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and the oases of Tuat and Gourara. Around 1250, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> ended, the Beni Hassan defeated the Sanhaja Berber confederation and took tribute from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the mid-15th century they controlled much of the oases and the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Taking advantage of the weakening Marinid Sultanate around 1460 they crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a>, and by the early 16th century they dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haouz">Haouz</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, where the Rahamna served the Saadian Sultanate. Other groups went north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> or settled south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, while part of the tribe moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>.</p><p>The Char Bouba War was fought against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> marabouts, who followed the doctrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-din-mauritanian-cleric">Nasr al-Din</a>. In 1673 Nasr al-Din invaded Futa Tooro and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> states beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a>, gaining the gum-trade ports along it. A scholar of Shinqit issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> denying that he was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> with the right to levy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a>. In 1674 the Beni Hassan defeated the marabouts and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of Trarza, Brakna, Tagant, Adrar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodh">Hodh</a>, which fought each other repeatedly and raided the Wolof of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> through the 18th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>After 1674 the defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes had to give up their arms and pay the horma tribute to the Arab warrior tribes. They became either religious tribes, the marabouts or zawiya, or the lower znaga, semi-sedentary farmers and fishermen. This order held until French colonisation broke the power of the Hassane, though the tribes' traditional roles remain socially important, and the hierarchy has led to oppression, discrimination and enslavement of other groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. In the 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> led the cultural and linguistic Arabization of Mauritania, and the Sahrawi nation counts the Beni Hassan among its founding peoples.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beni Ḥassān” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Awlad Mbarak were a Hassani warrior group, part of the Arab <b>Bani Hassan</b> who had spread across the western Sahara.</blockquote><a href="/state/awlad-mbarak-emirate">Awlad Mbarak emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Brakna emirate emerged in the 17th century in south-central Mauritania, as <b>Hassani Arab</b> warrior groups established dominance over Berber scholarly tribes after the Char Bouba war of the 1670s.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-brakna">Emirate of Brakna</a></li><li><blockquote>The Trarza emirate took shape in the 17th century among <b>Hassani Arab</b> groups of south-western Mauritania, ruled by the Awlad Ahmed min Daman lineage.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-trarza">Emirate of Trarza</a></li><li><blockquote>Nasir al-Din was a cleric of the Zawaya, the Berber scholarly tribes of south-western Mauritania, who resented the dominance of <b>Hassani Arab</b> warriors and the slave trade with the French at Saint-Louis.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-nasir-al-din">Imamate of Nasir al-Din</a></li></ul></section>
